# Local configuration for the FBC reviewer. Copy to `secrets/local.env`.
#
#     cp secrets/local.env.example secrets/local.env
#
# `secrets/local.env` is git-ignored and must stay that way — it holds a mail
# password and an API key. This example file carries no values and is committed
# so the variable names live in the repository rather than in somebody's head.
#
# Read by two paths, which is why the format is this strict:
#   * scripts/share.sh and share.ps1 pass it to `docker run --env-file`
#   * a bare `uvicorn webapp.server:app` reads it through webapp/envfile.py
#
# THE RULES
#   * KEY=value, one per line. `#` starts a comment line.
#   * DO NOT QUOTE VALUES. Docker's --env-file takes quotes literally, so
#     FBC_SMTP_PASS="abcd efgh" would send the quotes as part of the password.
#     A password with spaces in it needs no quoting here — write it bare.
#   * No $VAR expansion. The value is exactly the characters after the `=`.
#   * Anything already set in the environment wins over this file.

# ── mail ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What it turns on: the review delivered by email when one is requested, an
# immediate note to the owners when feedback escalates, and the digest that
# `/admin` can send on demand. Without it all three are skipped and the
# feedback still queues — you just have to go and look at /admin.
#
# Google Workspace: smtp.gmail.com on 587 with STARTTLS, which is what
# webapp/mailer.py does. FBC_SMTP_USER is the full address, and the password
# is a 16-character App Password, NOT the account password — Google rejects
# the account password for SMTP on any account with 2-step verification, which
# is every Workspace account worth having. Generate one at
# https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords while signed in as this account.
#
# FBC_MAIL_FROM must be the authenticated address or one of its verified
# aliases. Gmail silently rewrites, or rejects, a From it does not own.
FBC_SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
FBC_SMTP_PORT=587
FBC_SMTP_USER=
FBC_SMTP_PASS=
FBC_MAIL_FROM=

# Who gets escalations and the digest, and who may approve a calibration
# change. Comma-separated. Deliberately separate from FBC_ALLOWED_EMAILS:
# being allowed to run a review is not being allowed to re-level a rule for
# everyone. Empty means nobody, which is the right default.
FBC_OWNER_EMAILS=

# ── the Anthropic key: the comment assist, and AI sheet reading ───────────
# One key, two separate uses. Setting the key turns on the first. The second
# also needs FBC_AI_READING=on below, because a key that is here for the assist
# is not consent to send client drawings to an API.
#
# 1. THE COMMENT ASSIST. The free-text half of a feedback submission is read
#    and summarised before it reaches the queue, so an escalation arrives with
#    a sentence saying what the person actually claimed. It runs after a review
#    has finished, on a background thread, and never reaches a review:
#    tests/test_training.py walks the import graph from webapp.worker and
#    fbcreview and fails if webapp.assist is reachable from either. Its opinion
#    is advisory and one-directional: it may raise a disposition and may never
#    lower one. Unset, an unstructured comment routes to a person unread.
#
# 2. AI SHEET READING (only with FBC_AI_READING=on). See the next section.
#
# Keys are at https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys and start `sk-ant-`.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Only for an IDENTITY-LINKED key, and required for one.
#
# The console issues two kinds. A *workspace* key is bound to one workspace and
# needs nothing here. An *identity-linked* key belongs to the person or service
# account that created it, can act in more than one workspace, and so will not
# act at all until the request names one — the API answers
#
#   400  anthropic-workspace-id is required when authenticating with an
#        identity-linked API key
#
# The key type is in the console's "Type" column. The workspace id starts
# `wrkspc_` and is on the workspace's own page under Organization settings →
# Workspaces. It is an identifier rather than a credential, but it lives here
# because it is useless apart from the key.
#
# Leave empty for a workspace key: an empty header is a 400 of its own, so it
# is only sent when set.
ANTHROPIC_WORKSPACE_ID=

# ── AI sheet reading (optional; off unless switched on) ───────────────────
# What it turns on: each sheet of an uploaded set is also read by Claude — an
# image of the sheet, crops of any pasted raster tables, and its text layer —
# and the model proposes where each value the rules need is printed. Nothing it
# proposes is used until fbcreview/ai/grounding.py has found the quoted text on
# the sheet and parsed the value out of it; everything else is recorded as
# rejected and never reaches a rule. The rules, thresholds and code citations
# stay pure Python. CLAUDE.md, "AI reads; rules decide", has the guardrails.
#
# WHAT LEAVES THE MACHINE: the sheet images and text of every set reviewed go
# to the Anthropic API. Turn it on only where that is acceptable for the sets
# this deployment reviews.
#
# Off, or on with the API unreachable, the review is the deterministic one — it
# never fails because a model did. A re-run replays the first run's readings
# (stored beside findings.json as readings.json) rather than paying again.
#
# Server-side refusal fallbacks are on: if the configured model declines a
# sheet on policy grounds, the API re-runs that one request on the model
# Anthropic recommends for it. A sheet declined anyway is just not AI-read.
FBC_AI_READING=off
# Model id. Empty means the default in fbcreview/ai/reader.py (claude-opus-5).
FBC_AI_MODEL=
# low | medium | high | xhigh | max. How hard the model thinks per sheet.
# Empty (or anything else) means medium: reading a label and its value off a
# sheet is transcription, not reasoning, and the grounding check — not the
# effort level — is what stops a misread from reaching a rule.
FBC_AI_EFFORT=
# Sheets read in parallel. Empty means 6.
FBC_AI_CONCURRENCY=
# Sheets past this many are not AI-read (the deterministic reader still reads
# them). Empty means 60.
FBC_AI_MAX_SHEETS=
# Seconds per sheet request, and for the whole set. Empty means 240 and 900.
# Past the set deadline the review carries on with whatever has been read.
FBC_AI_TIMEOUT_S=
FBC_AI_DEADLINE_S=
# Where readings are cached by file hash + model + prompt version, so the same
# PDF uploaded twice is read once. Empty means a folder under the system temp
# directory. The cache holds sheet text: keep it on this machine.
FBC_AI_CACHE_DIR=

# The result check (only with FBC_AI_READING=on). After the rules run, Claude
# reviews the result against the review options and the sheets' text and
# corrects it: it revises, adds or withdraws findings (each labelled on the
# finding; the weighty ones need a quote printed on the sheet) and can send
# sheets back to be read. Pass 1 checks, pass 2 edits, pass 3 verifies.
# Empty means on. "off" keeps AI reading and skips the check.
FBC_AI_REVIEW=
# 1-3. Empty means 3. Anything above 3 is held at 3.
FBC_AI_MAX_PASSES=
# Empty means the reader's model (FBC_AI_MODEL).
FBC_AI_REVIEW_MODEL=
# low | medium | high | xhigh | max. Empty means high: one request per pass,
# and judging a whole result is reasoning, not transcription.
FBC_AI_REVIEW_EFFORT=

# ── issues from escalated feedback (optional) ─────────────────────────────
# With both set, an escalation can be opened as a GitHub issue from /admin.
# Inert without them, exactly like mail.
FBC_GITHUB_REPO=
FBC_GITHUB_TOKEN=
